  "worked_solution": "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)-k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)-kt){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )-\\pi k-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0) \\\\  & =-\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi k & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
